course: dictionary
lesson: 1383
title: Capsaicin: why chillies burn
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Capsaicin is a natural chemical found in chilli peppers. It is why chillies taste hot. «Chillies contain capsaicin, which makes the mouth feel heat.» The sentence tells you what capsaicin does: it makes your mouth feel hot.
explain: Your mouth has nerves that sense heat. Capsaicin tricks these nerves, so your mouth feels hot and burning, even though nothing in your mouth is really on fire. That is why a chilli can sting even when it is not hot to touch.
fact: Capsaicin is a natural chemical found in chilli peppers.
fact: Capsaicin tricks the nerves that sense heat, so your mouth feels hot even though nothing is really burning.
fact: Milk calms the burn better than water does.
fact: Birds cannot feel the burn of capsaicin, so they can eat chillies and spread the seeds.
fact: Capsaicin is used in some creams for aches, and the heat of chillies is measured on the Scoville scale.
exercise: «Chillies contain capsaicin, which makes the mouth feel heat.» What does capsaicin make your mouth feel? | Cold and frosty | Wet, like water | Sweet and sticky | *Hot, as if it is burning
exercise: «Chillies contain capsaicin, which tricks pain sensors into feeling heat; mammals avoid chillies.» Which animals avoid chillies? | Milk | Chilli seeds | *Mammals | Birds
exercise: Which drink calms the burn of capsaicin better? | Fizzy lemonade | *Milk | Water | Juice
test: What is the Scoville scale used to measure? | The colour of chilli peppers | The weight of chilli seeds | *How hot chillies are | The length of chillies
test: Capsaicin is used in some creams for which problem? | Cleaning floors | Growing plants | *Aches | Making sweets
exam: Why does a chilli make your mouth feel hot even though nothing is burning? | Chilli seeds are hot to touch | Your mouth is really on fire | Birds spread the heat around | *Capsaicin tricks the heat nerves in your mouth
